          %title The trace of a category
          %h1#the_trace_of_a_category The trace of a category
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#finset"} FinSet
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#others"} Others
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The trace of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (or more generally of an endo
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            or endo
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"}> profunctor
            ) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            of a linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
            on a finite dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            (that is a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrix
          %p
            A notion of trace is generally definable for maps in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            (even more generally in a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+category"} traced monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
          %p
            As an instance of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcosm+principle"} microcosm principle
            %em universal trace
            function for morphisms
            %em in
            that category, and also supplies the necessary structure to define
            %span.newWikiWord
              bicategorical traces
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/trace+in+a+bicategory"} ?
            in the bicategory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a compact closed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            and monoidal unit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %strong trace
            of an endomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: c \to c
            is the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi η
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi c
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi c
                            %mo *
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi c
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi ε
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \overset{\eta}{\to} c^* \otimes c \overset{1_{c^*} \otimes f}{\to} c^* \otimes c \overset{\varepsilon}{\to} 1
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is a unit and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            is a counit of appropriate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            (note that the symmetry makes the dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^*
            both a right and left adjoint of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the category of finite-dimensional vector spaces with its usual monoidal structure, this gives the usual trace of an endomorphism; in particular, for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = 1_c
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim(c) \in hom(1, 1)
          %p
            The same idea applies to compact closed symmetric monoidal bicategories. In particular, it applies to the bicategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
            whose objects are small categories, whose 1-morphisms are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: D^{op} \times C \to Set
          %p
            and whose 2-morphisms are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            between profunctors. The bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prof
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+bicategory"} cartesian bicategory
            and hence symmetric monoidal under
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            in this case is just the opposite category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi ε
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta: 1 \to C^{op} \times C, \, \varepsilon: C^{op} \times C \to 1
          %p
            are given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom_{C^{op}}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom_C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            formula for profunctor composition) yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi c
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int^{c, c' \in Ob(C)} hom(c, c') \times hom(c', c) \cong \int^c hom(c, c)
          %p
            and this is the trace of the identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_C
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prof
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof
            (which as a functor is also given by
            = succeed ");" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom_C
            %strong trace
            of the category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %strong dimension
            of the category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the coend
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int^{c \in Ob(C)} F(c, c)
          %p which generalizes the trace of linear functions:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr(f) = \sum_i f_{i i}
          %p
            (where the matrix entries
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{i j}
            are computed with respect to any basis).
          %p
            The foregoing discussion can be generalized to the case of bimodules between small categories enriched in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+closed+category"} monoidal closed category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the object of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            given by the enriched coend
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi c
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int^c hom(c, c)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#finset FinSet
          %p
            We calculate the trace or dimension of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: c \to c
            between finite sets, modulo the equivalence relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            generated by the stipulation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ g \sim g \circ f
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: c \to d
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: d \to c
            are functions between finite sets.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: c \to c
            be a finite endofunction, and let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi i
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \overset{p}{\to} d \overset{i}{\to} c
          %p
            be its epi-mono factorization. Then
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h = (i \circ p) \sim (p \circ i)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            as the image
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(c)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \circ i
            can be viewed as the restriction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {h|}\colon h(c) \to h(c)
          %p and this process iterates. The sequence of epis
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(c) \overset{h|}{\to} h^{(2)}(c) \overset{h|}{\to} \ldots
          %p
            eventually stabilizes (after finitely many steps) to a finite set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^{(\infty)}(c)
            on which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            restricts to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjective
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunction"} endofunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: c \to c
            is
            = succeed "-equivalent" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: d \to d
          %p
            Furthermore, given two permutations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: c \to c
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo :
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau: d \to d
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \sim \tau
            is witnessed by a chain of function equalities
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = g_1 f_1, \; f_1 g_1 = h_1 = g_2 f_2, \; \ldots, \; f_{n-1} g_{n-1} = h_{n-1} = g_n f_n, \; f_n g_n = \tau
          %p
            one may show (using
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi k
                  %msubsup
                    %mi h
                    %mi k
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_k h_k^j f_k = g_k (f_k g_k)^j f_k = (g_k f_k)^{j+1} = h_{k-1}^{j+1}
            and similarly
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi k
                    %msubsup
                      %mi h
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi h
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_k h_{k-1}^j g_k = h_k^{j+1}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi σ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi τ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1 g_2 \ldots g_{n-1} g_n f_n f_{n-1} \ldots f_2 f_1 = \sigma^n, \qquad f_n f_{n-1} \ldots f_2 f_1 g_1 g_2 \ldots g_{n-1} g_n = \tau^n
          %p
            which implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = f_n \ldots f_1
            is invertible and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau f = f \sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mi σ
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau = f \sigma f^{-1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mi σ
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau = f \sigma f^{-1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mi σ
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = \sigma f^{-1}
            we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = g f
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f g = \tau
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \sim \tau
          %p
            In other words, the trace of the category of finite sets is isomorphic to the trace of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} underlying groupoid
            of finite sets and bijections, where the equivalence classes with respect to
